-- Completion, asked at the positions an editor asks it - Implementation-Tooling.md §8.
--
-- Each caret marks the byte the cursor is *before*, which is how an editor's caret sits at a
-- column: a caret under the `l` of `scale` is a cursor that has typed `sca`. So this file is a
-- program that compiles, and the half-written names completion has to work on are made by putting
-- the cursor inside a finished one - which is also what a real request most often is, since a name
-- is edited far more often than it is first typed.
--
-- Every marker is answered by a compile of its own with the cursor set, so nothing one marker does
-- to the parse is visible to another.

module

import Shapes
import Hidden(hiddenPair)
import qualified Util as U

-- What follows a `.` is the receiver's fields, then every visible function a dot-call could reach -
-- §8.1's third kind, both halves. The first caret is on the field name's first byte, which is a
-- cursor that has typed the dot and nothing after it.
--
-- The ranking is what this marker is really about: the fields first, then this module's functions,
-- then everything reached through an import. A field is what the receiver *has* and a dot-call is
-- what the program can do to it - and where both answer to one name the field is what the resolver
-- selects, so it is what the list has to put first.
fn corner(p: Point) -> Int:
    let across = p.x
--                 ^member
    let down = p.y
--                 ^memberTyped

    return across + down

-- The receiver is followed through a reference exactly as field selection follows one.
fn shift(&p: Point) -> Int = p.y
--                             ^memberBorrow

-- A namespaced declaration reaches a dot only through the type that owns it, and under its last
-- segment - `Point.span` is offered here as `span`. `Corner.spanned` takes a `Point` too and is not
-- offered at all, which is what filtering this list on the prefix they share asserts: it would sit
-- directly beside `span` if the skip stopped working. See Shapes.yana.
fn spanning(p: Point) -> Int = p.span()
--                                   ^memberNamespace

-- The trigger character, and the one position where there is no token at the cursor to stand in
-- for: a `.` typed with nothing after it yet. The parser reaches the end of the statement instead,
-- and the sentinel goes at the cursor rather than over anything. This body does not parse, which is
-- the point - it is the state every member completion passes through - and the declarations around
-- it are unaffected, which is what M7 bought.
fn typing(p: Point) -> Int:
    p.
--    ^dot
    return p.x

-- Not exported, and offered all the same. A dot-call reaches every function this *body* can name,
-- which is what makes the form universal rather than a per-type namespace: the import half of the
-- visibility rule is asked of imports, and a private function of this module is not one.
fn privately(p: Point) -> Int = p.x - p.y

-- A multi-constructor receiver has no fields to offer - it has to be matched on before a field of
-- it means anything - and every dot-call a single-constructor one has. So the two halves are not
-- one answer: the fields are skipped here and the functions are not, which is why `area`, `scale`
-- and `shifted` are the whole of this list.
fn overShape(s: Shape) -> Int:
    let scaled = s.scale(2)
--                 ^memberSum

    return scaled

-- An expected type ranks the answer above everything that merely shares its prefix, and the
-- expected type of an argument is the parameter it fills.
fn pickInt(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int = if a > b then a else b

fn main() -> Int:
    let box = Square {side: 3}
    let boxScale = 2
    let boxed = scale(box, boxScale)
--                  ^callee   ^argument

    -- `shapeOrigin` produces what this ascription asked for and sorts above `Shape`, which shares
    -- its prefix and nothing else.
    let here = shapeOrigin() :: Point
--                ^expected

    -- The same for a constructor: `Nothing` is how the ascribed `Maybe` is written, and it carries
    -- nothing, so choosing it finishes the expression.
    let none = Nothing :: Maybe(Int)
--                ^constructor

    -- A constructor that does carry something takes the brackets its declaration was written with:
    -- named fields are a record and a bare payload is an argument. The carets are on names that
    -- nothing follows, because a constructor whose brackets are already there gets none added -
    -- which is what `alreadyCalled` below is about.
    let sq = Square {side: 4}
    let just = 1

    let shape = sq
--                ^namedFields
    let payload = just
--                   ^payload

    -- The caret inside a name that is already a call. The brackets are there, so completing must
    -- not add a second pair - which is what the rest of the name after the caret is read for.
    let twice = scaleTwice(box)
--                   ^alreadyCalled

    -- Reachable only under the alias it was imported as, so the item has to carry it.
    let extra = U.qualOnly()
--                     ^qualified

    -- An include list decides whether a name exists here at all: `hiddenTwin` is next to
    -- `hiddenPair` in its own module and is not offered.
    let pair = hiddenPair()
--                  ^included

    -- A line with nothing on it, which is the state a body is in the moment a place to write is
    -- opened. No production runs at the cursor, so the block itself has to notice - and what it
    -- offers is the scope as it stands *here*, which does not include `tail` below.

--  ^empty
    let tail = pickInt(boxed, pair)
    return here.x + extra + tail + twice + shape.side + payload + (none ?? 0)

-- Inside the braces of a construction, where the fields of what is being *written* are the answer -
-- §8.1's fourth kind. The two positions differ by one character of text and by what can be meant
-- there: a name in front of a `:` can only be a field, and one with nothing after it is a field
-- name half-typed or the value of a positional constructor, so the scope comes with it under the
-- fields.
--
-- Their own declarations, on the same terms as `typing` above: neither body compiles, because a
-- half-written field name is not a field, and a body that does not compile takes the rest of its
-- own statements with it.
fn writingField() -> Shape:
    return Square {si}
--                   ^field

fn writingNamed() -> Shape:
    return Square {sid: 5}
--                    ^fieldName

-- The same for a tuple, whose fields are known here because the position expects one. Without an
-- expected type there is nothing to complete against - the tuple's type is about to be made out of
-- what the author writes rather than known in advance.
fn writingTuple() -> Int:
    return shapeAt({x: 1, y})
--                        ^tupleField

-- A record whose type and whose constructor have the same name, which is every `data` with one
-- constructor. One name is one item, so the order the tables are walked in decides which of the two
-- it is - and in an expression the constructor is the only one of them that is a value, which is
-- also the only one of them that knows to open braces.
--
-- Its own declaration, and the one below it too, for the reason the three above have one: a name
-- with nothing after it is not a name, and the statements under it would go with it.
fn naming() -> Point = Poin
--                       ^recordName

-- A constructor whose fields are not all required. `weight` has a default, so writing it is
-- optional and a placeholder for it would be something to delete rather than something to fill in.
fn tagging() -> Label = Labe
--                        ^defaulted

-- The same distinction at a call: `by` has a default, so the snippet stops at `s` and the detail
-- says what the position would be filled with.
fn shifting(s: Shape) -> Int = shifte
--                                  ^defaultedCall

-- An argument offers the *parameter names* of the call it is in as well as the names in scope: it
-- may be written either way, and which parameter a value fills is what an author who cannot
-- remember the order is reaching for. A parameter called `s` and a local called `s` are both here,
-- because one is not a shadow of the other - `s: ` names a position and `s` passes a value.
fn arguments(s: Shape) -> Int = shifted(s)
--                                      ^argumentName

-- A cursor in the *name* half of a pair can be nothing but a parameter, so nothing else comes.
fn argumentNaming(s: Shape) -> Int = shifted(s: s)
--                                           ^namePosition

-- A constructor in a pattern - §8.1's fifth kind. What ranks first is the constructors of the
-- *pivot*, which is what makes the answer about the value being matched rather than about every
-- record in the program; the rest follow. A lowercase name in the same position is a binding the
-- author is introducing, so nothing is offered for one.
fn matching(s: Shape) -> Int = match s:
    Squ -> 1
--     ^pattern
    _ -> 0

-- An update path, which is field selection with a value on the end of it - so what it offers is
-- what a `.` offers, one step at a time. The second caret is a segment nobody has typed yet, and
-- the third is inside a *later* segment, where the answer is the fields of the one before it.
fn moving(l: Line) -> Line = {l | fro: origin()}
--                                   ^update

fn moved(l: Line) -> Line = {l | }
--                               ^updateEmpty

fn nested(l: Line) -> Line = {l | .from.}
--                                      ^updatePath

fn origin() -> Point = Point {x: 0, y: 0}
